title: Correlating the GUI and the daemon
tags: [[Slow log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The GUI times every daemon call and sends an id and what the user typed, so its entries line up with the daemon's.

The GUI times every call it makes to the daemon, at its single chokepoint (the
daemon proxy: the WebView cannot reach the daemon directly, so one place sees
them all). A call slower than the GUI's own threshold
([[Slow log configuration]]) is logged with `source: "gui"`, the operation it
asked for, and one `http` phase.

Two optional headers, which the daemon ignores when absent, tie the two sides
together:

* `X-Metafolder-Op-Id` — a short opaque id (the GUI sends 16 hex characters), stored as the entry's `op_id` on both sides. A GUI entry of 4.9 s against a daemon entry of 0.2 s for the same id is the diagnosis: the daemon was fine, the time went to the client.
* `X-Metafolder-Context` — what the ''user'' asked for, in their words: the DSL text of a query, the command that ran. The daemon receives the query IR, not the text that produced it, so it cannot reconstruct this. It lands in `context.client`.

The daemon keeps at most 200 characters of either header.

A panel declares its context with `metafolder.daemon.setContext(text)` — a local
assignment, no call — and it rides along with that panel's calls until replaced;
the `metarecord-list` panel sets it to the DSL text whenever it compiles a query.
It is a hint for whoever reads the log, not an exact attribution: a call made
just after a query carries that query's text too.

The GUI does not display the log; it is read with `mf slow`. A panel would be a
reasonable addition, but the log's job is to survive until someone looks, not to
be looked at continuously.
